# Superpowers brainstorm server scratch (pids, server state, generated html).
.superpowers/brainstorm/

# Superpowers SDD workspaces: per-plan ledgers, briefs, reports, and review
# diffs. These are scratch for one plan's execution — the review diffs
# regenerate from git, and the reports describe work whose real record is the
# commits themselves.
#
# These used to be tracked "by repo convention", but the convention only ever
# covered three files at the flat legacy path, while every workspace created
# since writes its own `*` .gitignore into a per-plan subdirectory. So the
# convention had already stopped holding in practice; untracking the
# stragglers makes the rule uniform rather than changing it. History keeps
# them, and `git rm --cached` leaves them on disk.
.superpowers/sdd/

# Local research corpus dir (the 1,551-paper superset of sample-markdown-corpus/)
/markdown-corpus

# npm (lingrep-web/editor vendored bundle build)
node_modules/

# Python adapter environments and bytecode caches
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# lingrep-web derived corpus-cache artifacts (rebuildable; never committed)
*.provenance.sqlite
*.provenance.sqlite-wal
*.provenance.sqlite-shm
*.provenance.sqlite.building
*.provenance.manifest.json

# Immutable lingrep V1 corpus artifacts (rebuildable; never committed). The published
# form is a directory beside the input it was built from,
# `<input>.g<generation>.<identity>.lingcorpus`, so it lands wherever a corpus
# lives rather than under a single artifact root. An interrupted build leaves a
# `.<name>.staging-<pid>-<sequence>` sibling. Both are large — a 162 MB JSONL
# corpus publishes ~1 GB — and both regenerate from source on demand.
*.lingcorpus/
*.lingcorpus.staging-*/

# Raw research corpora are kept on the filesystem beside their prepared
# artifacts, too large for git; fetched or rebuilt per environment.
data-archive/bccwj.txt
data-archive/science-corpus.jsonl
data-archive/corpus-2000.jsonl
data-archive/corpus.jsonl
data-archive/sample-science.jsonl
data-archive/bench-corpus.jsonl
# The 100-paper Markdown dialect sample: research text, not redistributable.
/sample-markdown-corpus/

# insta's pending snapshots. `cargo insta` writes one beside the accepted
# `.snap` whenever an assertion moves, and `git add -A` then sweeps it into a
# commit -- which has now happened twice (c2f2942d, 1279a01e), the second time
# at 7,847 lines. Accepting a snapshot rewrites the `.snap`; the `.new` is
# never the artifact anyone wants tracked.
*.snap.new
